<Wiki
  title="Install"
  description="Install the latest PUV release on Linux, macOS, or Windows, or build the binary from source."
>
  <p class="kicker">Wiki</p>
  <h1>Install</h1>
  <p>
    One command per platform. Both scripts pick the archive for this machine from the latest
    GitHub release of <a href="https://github.com/haasele/puv">haasele/puv</a> and install it to
    <code>$&#123;PUV_INSTALL_DIR:-$HOME/.local/bin&#125;</code>. On Windows that directory is
    <code>%USERPROFILE%\.local\bin</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Linux and macOS</h2>
  <pre class="block">curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/haasele/puv/main/install.sh | sh</pre>
  <p>
    The script is
    <a href="https://github.com/haasele/puv/blob/main/install.sh"><code>install.sh</code></a>
    on <code>main</code>. Git Bash, MSYS, and Cygwin on Windows can use this command too. It
    installs <code>puv.exe</code> there.
  </p>

  <h2>Windows</h2>
  <pre class="block">irm https://raw.githubusercontent.com/haasele/puv/main/install.ps1 | iex</pre>
  <p>
    Run that in PowerShell. The script is
    <a href="https://github.com/haasele/puv/blob/main/install.ps1"><code>install.ps1</code></a>
    on <code>main</code>. It copies <code>puv.exe</code> and adds that directory to the user
    <code>PATH</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Release files</h2>
  <p>
    Release builds are published from the Actions tab: Release, then Run workflow. Both installers
    read whatever GitHub currently marks as the latest release. Each archive contains one binary,
    <code>puv</code>, or <code>puv.exe</code> on Windows.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>File</th><th>Machine</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>puv-x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu.tar.gz</td><td>Linux, glibc, 64-bit Intel/AMD</td></tr>
      <tr><td>puv-aarch64-unknown-linux-gnu.tar.gz</td><td>Linux, glibc, 64-bit ARM</td></tr>
      <tr><td>puv-x86_64-apple-darwin.tar.gz</td><td>macOS, 64-bit Intel</td></tr>
      <tr><td>puv-aarch64-apple-darwin.tar.gz</td><td>macOS, 64-bit ARM</td></tr>
      <tr><td>puv-x86_64-pc-windows-msvc.tar.gz</td><td>Windows, 64-bit Intel/AMD</td></tr>
      <tr><td>puv-aarch64-pc-windows-msvc.tar.gz</td><td>Windows, 64-bit ARM</td></tr>
      <tr><td>sha256sums.txt</td><td><code>sha256sum</code> output: the hash, two spaces, then the file name</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    PHP runtimes are a separate download. They are prebuilt StaticPHP CLI builds for Linux glibc
    (<code>x86_64</code> and <code>aarch64</code>), not a compile of php-src. See
    <a href="/wiki/commands/use"><code>puv use</code></a> and
    <a href="/wiki/commands/php"><code>puv php</code></a>.
  </p>

  <h2>From source</h2>
  <p>Rust 1.92 or newer. The workspace lockfile is the one to build.</p>
  <pre class="block">git clone https://github.com/haasele/puv
cd puv
cargo install --path crates/puv --locked</pre>
  <p>
    <code>cargo install</code> puts the binary in Cargo’s bin directory, usually
    <code>~/.cargo/bin</code>, not <code>~/.local/bin</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>After install</h2>
  <pre class="block">puv --version</pre>
  <p>
    The next page is a <a href="/wiki/new-project">new project</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Remove</h2>
  <p>Linux and macOS:</p>
  <pre class="block">rm "$&#123;PUV_INSTALL_DIR:-$HOME/.local/bin&#125;/puv"</pre>
  <p>Windows PowerShell, or <code>puv.exe</code> under <code>PUV_INSTALL_DIR</code> when that was set:</p>
  <pre class="block">Remove-Item "$env:USERPROFILE\.local\bin\puv.exe"</pre>
  <p>
    That deletes the binary. Cache and installed runtimes stay.
    <a href="/wiki/commands/prune"><code>puv prune --global</code></a> drops cache entries nothing
    references. <a href="/wiki/commands/php"><code>puv php remove</code></a> removes runtimes.
  </p>
</Wiki>
